**Yes. There are two relevant kinds:**

1. **Distributed balanced k-means:** Bateni et al., *Distributed Balanced Clustering via Mapping Coresets* (2014). It provides distributed approximation algorithms, but uses a **MapReduce model**, rather than communication only between geographic neighbors. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="0"}

2. **Spatially local balancing:** Pavone et al., *Equitable Partitioning Policies for Robotic Networks* (2009). Agents maintain **weighted Voronoi cells**, called power cells. Each agent adjusts its weight using neighboring cells’ loads, changing boundaries until workloads equalize. They prove convergence under their assumptions. **This is closer to your intended protocol.** :chatgpt-content-reference{index="1"}

An attractive feature: the planar cell-adjacency graph has **fewer than six neighbors on average**, although an individual cell can have more. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="2"}

The main qualification: their balancing theorem concerns a **continuous demand density in a convex domain**. Applying it to our finite pickup dots requires an explicit adaptation. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="3"}
